---
import Authors from "../../components/Authors.astro";
import Flow from "../../components/Flow.astro";
import GuardedReplay from "../../components/GuardedReplay.astro";
import CaseLayout from "../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro";
import { publications, research } from "../../content";
import { refs } from "../../content/refs";

const item = research.find((entry) => entry.id === "guarded-orchestration");
const paper = publications.find((entry) => entry.id === "guarded-orchestration-works26");
if (!item || !paper) throw new Error("Guarded orchestration content is missing");
const repo = refs.pcorona;
const sections = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "hard", label: "Why it is hard" },
  { id: "role", label: "My role" },
  { id: "approach", label: "The approach" },
  { id: "conversation", label: "A conversation" },
  { id: "decisions", label: "Decisions" },
  { id: "results", label: "Results" },
  { id: "cite", label: "Links and citation" },
];
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Guarded orchestration for scientific models · Nieb Hasan Neom"
  description={item.tagline}
  heading="Guarded orchestration for scientific models"
  lede={item.tagline}
  sections={sections}
>
  <dl class="facts" slot="facts">
    <div><dt>Status</dt><dd class:list={["status", `tone-${item.tone}`]}>{item.status}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Paper</dt><dd>{paper.title}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Authors</dt><dd><Authors authors={paper.authors} /></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Venue</dt><dd><a href={refs.works26}>WORKS26</a>, held with <a href={refs.sc26}>SC26</a> in Chicago on 15 November 2026</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Code</dt><dd class="links"><a href={repo}>GitHub ↗</a></dd></div>
  </dl>

  <section id="problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Scientists publish trained prediction models so others can reuse them, but the models usually arrive as code, notebooks, or command-line tools. Using one means knowing the exact input names, types, and units. A language model in front of the model could take a plain-language description instead, but function calling only gives it a way to call a tool. It does not say when a scientific input is complete, or what a correction should change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hard">
    <h2>Why it is hard</h2>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Inputs often arrive over several turns, and a missing value has to stop the run rather than be guessed.</li>
      <li>Every value must reach the model with the right name and type.</li>
      <li>When a scientist corrects one input or asks a what-if question, nothing else may change; when they start a new sample, nothing old may carry over.</li>
      <li>A fluent answer from the language model is not evidence that the model ran.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <p>
      I am the first author. I designed the guarded, state-aware workflow and its execution rules, and instantiated it for protein-corona prediction over a backend of trained prediction models, with my co-authors M. R. Gartia and <a href={refs.advisor}>M. Arifuzzaman</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="approach">
    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The workflow splits the work between the language model and the service around it. The language model interprets: it collects values from the conversation, narrates results, and recognizes what a follow-up means. The service enforces: it checks the inputs, runs the backend, and holds the state that later steps build on.
    </p>
    <Flow
      label="Guarded orchestration workflow"
      legend
      steps={[
        { title: "Collect", text: "Gather inputs across turns and ask for anything missing.", role: "model" },
        { title: "Guard", text: "Translate names, then recheck that every input is present and typed.", role: "service" },
        { title: "Execute", text: "Run the trained model once and store the accepted inputs.", role: "service" },
        { title: "Narrate", text: "Explain the returned result in the scientist's terms.", role: "model" },
        { title: "Steer", text: "Read a follow-up as a correction, a what-if, or a new sample.", role: "model" },
      ]}
      loop="A correction or what-if changes one input and keeps the rest; a new sample clears everything."
      caption="Redrawn for this site from the paper's workflow."
    />
    <p>
      For protein-corona prediction the model needs ten descriptors: base material, surface modification, TEM size, zeta potential, protein source, plasma concentration, nanoparticle concentration, and centrifugation speed, time, and temperature.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="conversation">
    <h2>A conversation</h2>
    <p>Step through a session to watch the inputs fill in, the backend stay blocked until all ten are present, and what-if, correction, and reset each change the state differently.</p>
    <GuardedReplay />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li><b>Enforce completeness in the service, not the prompt.</b> The prompt tells the language model when to call the tool, but the service checks the inputs again before anything runs. Nothing incomplete reaches the backend, whatever the model decides.</li>
      <li><b>Give every follow-up an explicit meaning.</b> A correction and a what-if each change one value and keep the others; a new sample starts from nothing. The rules live in the workflow, not in the model's judgment.</li>
      <li><b>Keep the language model out of the science.</b> It interprets and explains, but every prediction comes from the trained model, returned as data before it is narrated.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>The results are in the WORKS26 paper. A link to the paper will be added here when the proceedings are online.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cite">
    <h2>Links and citation</h2>
    <p class="links"><a href={repo}>Code on GitHub ↗</a><a href="/publications/#guarded-orchestration-works26">Citation</a></p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
